  "output": "Technical Analysis of Queue Incident: Dependency B Rate Limiting and Retry Amplification\\n\\nIntroduction\\nThis technical analysis examines a queueing incident that occurred between 09:00 and 09:46. The objective is to determine the root cause of a significant surge in queue depth and task latency, distinguishing the primary driver from secondary amplifiers. Based on the provided synthetic evidence (E01-E10), the incident was characterized by a sudden breakdown in processing efficiency, resulting in a queue depth increase of approximately 1,441% within ten minutes. This analysis concludes that the primary cause was an external rate limit imposed by Dependency B, which was significantly exacerbated by an internal retry policy that created a feedback loop of congestion.\\n\\nTimeline and Telemetry Analysis\\nAt the start of the observation window (09:00), the system was operating at a baseline state with a queue depth of 120 and 8 active workers (E01). However, by 09:10, the queue depth escalated sharply to 1,850 (E02). This sudden accumulation of pending tasks was accompanied by a severe degradation in performance; by 09:12, the median task latency had risen from 1.8 seconds to 14.2 seconds, a nearly eight-fold increase (E03). To determine if this was caused by internal resource exhaustion, system telemetry was reviewed at 09:14. The data showed CPU utilization at 43%, memory at 51%, and disk utilization at 22% (E04). These metrics indicate that the system had ample headroom and was not suffering from compute, memory, or I/O bottlenecks. This effectively rules out internal resource saturation as the primary cause of the latency and queue growth.\\n\\nThe Role of Dependency B\\nThe critical turning point in the incident occurred at 09:15, when Dependency B began returning HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) responses for 31% of all outbound requests (E05). An HTTP 429 status code is an explicit signal from a server that the client has exceeded its allocated request quota. This represents the primary cause of the incident. Because nearly one-third of the tasks were failing immediately upon attempting to contact Dependency B, the processing pipeline stalled. Tasks that would normally complete in under two seconds were now either failing or entering a retry state, which directly contributed to the escalating queue depth.\\n\\nRetry Policy as an Amplifier\\nWhile Dependency B's rate limiting triggered the incident, the internal retry mechanism acted as a powerful amplifier. At 09:16, the retry volume increased by 420% (E06). The system was utilizing exponential backoff with a maximum of three retries. In a rate-limiting scenario, exponential backoff is intended to reduce load; however, when a large volume of tasks (31%) fails simultaneously, the sheer number of retried requests can create a 'retry storm.' This storm keeps tasks in the queue for much longer than their original TTL, occupying worker slots and preventing new tasks from being processed. The 420% increase in retry volume indicates that the system was spending more effort attempting to re-process failed requests than it was processing new ones, effectively clogging the pipeline.\\n\\nIneffectiveness of Horizontal Scaling\\nIn an attempt to mitigate the queue growth, the worker count was increased from 8 to 16 at 09:20 (E07). Despite doubling the processing capacity, the queue depth was reduced by only 6% over the following ten minutes. This outcome is highly revealing. In a compute-bound or memory-bound scenario, increasing workers typically yields a proportional increase in throughput. However, in this case, the bottleneck was external. Adding more workers simply meant more concurrent requests were hitting the rate limit of Dependency B, which likely sustained or even worsened the HTTP 429 frequency. The workers were not limited by their own capacity, but by the external service's willingness to accept traffic.\\n\\nMitigation and Resolution\\nA pivotal diagnostic step occurred at 09:26, when retries for Dependency B were temporarily disabled (E08). This action resulted in a 38% reduction in queue depth within just six minutes. Although this increase in direct failure rate was undesirable, it proved that the retry logic was the primary driver of the queue's size. By failing fast rather than retrying, the system was able to clear the backlog of 'zombie' tasks that were merely waiting for their next backoff window. \\n\\nFull recovery was achieved at 09:35 after the rate limits on Dependency B were raised (E09). Following this change, HTTP 429 responses fell below 1%, and the queue depth returned to a healthy level (below 150) within eleven minutes. The stability of the environment was further confirmed by the fact that no deployments, schema changes, or network modifications occurred between 08:00 and 10:00 (E10), ensuring that the incident was not a result of internal configuration drift or software regressions.\\n\\nAlternative Hypothesis Evaluation\\nOne plausible alternative hypothesis is that the incident was caused by a network partition or general connectivity failure between the service and Dependency B. A network failure would also explain the increase in latency (E03) and the rise in queue depth (E02). However, this is less supported by the evidence than the rate-limiting hypothesis. A general network failure typically manifests as TCP timeouts or HTTP 5xx (Server Error) responses. The specific observation of HTTP 429 responses (E05) is a clear, application-level signal of rate limiting. Furthermore, the fact that the incident was resolved specifically by raising rate limits (E09) rather than fixing a routing issue or restarting a gateway strongly supports the rate-limiting conclusion.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe evidence demonstrates a clear causal chain: Dependency B imposed rate limits (Primary Cause) -> 31% of requests failed with HTTP 429 -> The internal retry policy amplified this failure by 420% (Amplifier) -> The queue depth exploded due to task persistence and retry loops -> Horizontal scaling failed because the bottleneck was external. The recovery was only possible once the external constraint was lifted and the internal amplification (retries) was managed.",
